WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.241
Picture a company that runs post offices.

2
00:00:05.241 --> 00:00:12.057
Every branch puts last lesson's clerks at the counter, and the company gives each branch a rule book for jobs.

3
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:15.729
The rule book reads only the label on each job ticket.

4
00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:18.807
A job marked quick stays with the counter clerk.

5
00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:21.372
An unmarked job goes to the back office.

6
00:00:21.372 --> 00:00:26.053
And a job stamped helper gets a temporary helper, hired for that one job.

7
00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:29.137
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:29.137 --> 00:00:33.256
When I write a slow method, how does the framework know it's slow?

9
00:00:33.256 --> 00:00:34.504
Does it look inside?

10
00:00:34.504 --> 00:00:36.064
Keep your answer in mind.

11
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:46.001
That company is Quarkus, a Java framework for web services: programs that answer requests sent over HTTP, the protocol of the web.

12
00:00:46.001 --> 00:00:55.837
Its counter is Vert.x, from last lesson: every request is first read by a counter clerk, an event loop, which Quarkus calls an IO thread.

13
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:57.479
Let's map it.

14
00:00:57.479 --> 00:01:07.296
A job ticket is a request to an endpoint: a Java method that answers one kind of request, such as, what does parcel P-17 cost?

15
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.777
The counter clerk is the IO thread.

16
00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:16.411
The back office is the worker pool, threads that are allowed to wait: 200 of them on a 4-core machine.

17
00:01:16.411 --> 00:01:18.428
The helper is a virtual thread.

18
00:01:18.428 --> 00:01:21.095
And the label is your method's signature.

19
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.733
Let's shrink it to one small service, the price desk, with two endpoints.

20
00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:29.382
The first, price, returns a plain Price.

21
00:01:29.382 --> 00:01:32.096
The second, live, returns a Uni of Price.

22
00:01:32.096 --> 00:01:37.393
A Uni, from the Mutiny library that Quarkus uses, means: the answer comes later.

23
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.524
Which thread runs each one?

24
00:01:39.524 --> 00:01:40.451
Make a guess.

25
00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:48.375
price returns a plain value, so it goes to the back office, on a worker thread.

26
00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:54.347
live returns a Uni, so it stays at the counter, on the IO thread, with no hand-off at all.

27
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:56.729
That's the whole rule.

28
00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:05.466
A method that returns Uni, Multi, CompletionStage or Publisher, or a Kotlin suspend function, says: the answer comes later.

29
00:02:05.466 --> 00:02:07.242
It runs on the IO thread.

30
00:02:07.242 --> 00:02:09.444
Anything else runs on a worker.

31
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:11.633
Read it as a promise you make.

32
00:02:11.633 --> 00:02:13.992
A Uni says: this method won't block.

33
00:02:13.992 --> 00:02:18.711
Quarkus believes that promise completely, and saves the hop to a worker.

34
00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:20.487
Now the trap.

35
00:02:20.487 --> 00:02:25.988
Someone wants the archive endpoint to feel modern, so it returns a Uni too.

36
00:02:25.988 --> 00:02:30.390
Inside, it calls Uni.createFrom().item, with priceBook.find.

37
00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.131
And find is a database call, made the blocking way.

38
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:36.559
Which thread makes that database call?

39
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:40.140
The IO thread.

40
00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:46.489
item wraps a value that already exists, so find runs right there, while the method builds the Uni.

41
00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:51.541
The counter clerk is stuck, and every connection on his loop waits behind him.

42
00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:57.117
It's like writing quick on a forty minute job, in the hope that the label makes it quick.

43
00:02:57.117 --> 00:02:59.100
Labels don't make anything quick.

44
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.024
They only decide who gets stuck.

45
00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:03.398
And here the ticket picture breaks.

46
00:03:03.398 --> 00:03:06.985
A real clerk would notice a forty minute job marked quick.

47
00:03:06.985 --> 00:03:10.758
Quarkus never will, because it never looks inside the method.

48
00:03:10.758 --> 00:03:13.356
So, our shy question: no, it doesn't look.

49
00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:15.985
How bad is one stuck clerk?

50
00:03:15.985 --> 00:03:20.429
Quarkus runs one event loop per CPU, half as many as plain Vert.x.

51
00:03:20.429 --> 00:03:25.344
On a 4-core laptop, one stuck clerk stalls a quarter of your connections.

52
00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:28.795
Quarkus's floor manager is friendlier, though.

53
00:03:28.795 --> 00:03:33.497
After 2 seconds, his first warning already carries the stack trace.

54
00:03:33.497 --> 00:03:39.603
In the lesson's demo, it came at 2,681 milliseconds, pointing at your own line of code.

55
00:03:40.433 --> 00:03:42.587
The fix is to change the label.

56
00:03:42.587 --> 00:03:47.034
@Blocking sends a method to a worker even when it returns a Uni.

57
00:03:47.034 --> 00:03:52.592
@NonBlocking keeps a method on the IO thread even when it returns a plain value.

58
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.498
And watch out in Kotlin.

59
00:03:54.498 --> 00:03:57.752
A suspend function counts as non-blocking too.

60
00:03:57.752 --> 00:04:03.977
A blocking database call inside one blocks an event loop, exactly like one inside a Uni.

61
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.297
The third label is @RunOnVirtualThread: hire a temporary helper.

62
00:04:09.297 --> 00:04:18.360
The code stays plain and blocking, but it runs on a new virtual thread, a cheap thread that the JDK puts to sleep while it waits.

63
00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:22.910
So, 200 calls arrive, and each one waits 100 milliseconds.

64
00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.478
A back office of 20 workers, against a helper for every call.

65
00:04:27.478 --> 00:04:28.976
Guess the two times.

66
00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:41.323
In the lesson's demo, 20 workers took 1,037 milliseconds: ten rounds of 100, just as Little's law from lesson 1 predicts.

67
00:04:41.323 --> 00:04:45.952
The helpers took 113 milliseconds: one round, all at once.

68
00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:48.423
Sounds like a free lunch.

69
00:04:48.423 --> 00:04:52.927
But helpers are cheap to hire, and the database still has 20 chairs.

70
00:04:52.927 --> 00:04:58.093
200 helpers calling a pool of 20 database connections still queue at the pool.

71
00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.252
The back office's size was also its protection.

72
00:05:01.252 --> 00:05:08.916
So put a limit back in front of anything fragile, such as a semaphore: a counter of permits that makes extra callers wait.

73
00:05:09.733 --> 00:05:12.998
Here are the labels in code, Java and Kotlin.

74
00:05:12.998 --> 00:05:15.247
A plain value goes to a worker.

75
00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:18.149
Uni, or suspend, stays on the IO thread.

76
00:05:18.149 --> 00:05:21.124
@Blocking moves a Uni method to a worker.

77
00:05:21.124 --> 00:05:23.953
And @RunOnVirtualThread hires a helper.

78
00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:27.505
So, does Quarkus look inside your method?

79
00:05:27.505 --> 00:05:27.906
Never.

80
00:05:27.906 --> 00:05:32.514
The return type is a promise about blocking, and Quarkus believes it.

81
00:05:32.514 --> 00:05:38.191
Return Uni only from code that never waits, and put a limit in front of the database.

82
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.261
Quick check.

83
00:05:39.261 --> 00:05:43.784
A Kotlin suspend endpoint calls a blocking repository directly.

84
00:05:43.784 --> 00:05:45.865
Which thread makes that call?

85
00:05:48.533 --> 00:05:52.566
The IO thread, so every connection on that event loop waits.

86
00:05:52.566 --> 00:05:55.523
Add @Blocking, or use a non-blocking client.
